Task Instructions
Hi — this is Dale Whitfield, shop supervisor at North River Foundry Works. The signed repair release for wooden pattern P-188 came through this morning, and I need the corrective work set up before P-188 goes back on the molding schedule. Can you take it the rest of the way? Everything's staged on the bench workstation: the signed release (dated 2026-07-22), our current signed bench-stock count, our Odoo purchasing system, and the pattern's maintenance record.

The release authorizes a specific set of repairs and, for each one, the materials and gross quantities it calls for. It also logs some surface-wear observations the inspector noted — we record those for history, but they don't authorize any purchasing this cycle, so keep them out of the ordering side.

One more thing: I may follow up with one or more signed addenda to the release after you're underway. Each one supersedes only the specific line item it names — everything else in the release, and in any earlier addenda, stands exactly as authorized. Make sure anything you've already set up ends up reflecting the most recently authorized figure for that item, and leave everything an addendum doesn't mention untouched.

Three things need to be true when you hand this back to me:

1. The supply order. For every authorized material where our current signed bench stock won't cover what the release requires, we need to buy the shortfall from Hearthline Pattern Supply, our approved pattern-materials vendor. Put those on a single draft request for quotation in Odoo — one line per short material, at the quantities we'd actually purchase (in Hearthline's purchase units and whatever pack or box sizes they sell in) and at their current quoted prices, with the RFQ pointing back to the P-188 repair release as its source. Materials the bench already covers don't go on the order at all. Our records aren't perfectly tidy — there's more than one stock count on file and the vendor and product lists carry a few look-alikes — so before anything lands on the RFQ, satisfy yourself that you're pricing current, approved Hearthline items against the current signed count and nothing else.

2. The service record. Add the current inspection-and-repair entry to P-188's maintenance record, dated 2026-07-22, capturing the inspection findings and the full authorized repair scope, including each material's gross requirement from the release — the full amount the repair calls for, not the reduced amount we ended up purchasing. This is our running history, so extend the record that's already open and leave every earlier entry exactly as it stands; don't start a fresh copy or work off a duplicate.

3. The RFQ reference, staged for my sign-off. I approve the procurement identifier before it becomes part of the record, so don't commit the new RFQ's identifier into the document yet. Instead, leave it staged in the maintenance record's Fields dialog as a Set Reference definition named P188_RFQ whose value is the identifier of the draft RFQ you created in Odoo — populated and ready to insert, but not inserted. Until I approve it, that identifier and that reference must not appear anywhere in the saved document or its live text; it should exist only as that pending definition.

Hand it back with the maintenance record open in LibreOffice Writer and the P188_RFQ definition sitting pending in its Fields dialog, and with the draft RFQ left in Odoo. Thanks.
